      "id": 1,
      "text": "Ames, Kansas was founded in 1883, long before the town’s first church appeared in 1872.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://kids.kiddle.co/Ames,_Kansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Ames sat on the south side of the Republican River, right on the Central Branch of the Union Pacific Railroad.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://legendsofkansas.com/ames-kansas/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Ames’s first store was run by George Clark, followed by a blacksmith, a general merchandiser, and a grocer.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://legendsofkansas.com/ames-kansas/",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Ames State Bank opened in September 1885, with W.D. Rice as president and E.K. as vice president.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://legendsofkansas.com/ames-kansas/",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The first newspaper, the Ames Advance, ran from 1885 to 1886, followed by the Ames Bureau in 1887.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://legendsofkansas.com/ames-kansas/",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "On April 27, 1886, a fire destroyed eight buildings and the entire lumber yard of the B.L. Lumber Company.",
